- and this in turn tended to be generalised to the singular. A possible reason is the a-forms’ phonetic similarity to war, waren. The final -e in wurde is unetymological; it goes back to a development in Early Modern German where -e was added to strong past tenses (after the subjunctive and/or by hypercorrection; hence obsolete sahe, fande for sah, fand). It may have survived here by analogy with the fellow auxiliary hatte."]],"senses":[[14089,"wurde","verb","first/third-person singular preterite of werden",0]],"inflections":[["wurde","werden","past"]]},"herhaltet":{"inflections":[["herhaltet","herhalten","indicative,plural,present,second-person,subordinate-clause"]]},"reichshofrats":{"inflections":[["reichshofrats","reichshofrat","genitive"]]}}}